# AeroShield dashboard configuration.
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Only .env.local is gitignored (see ./.gitignore); this template is committed and
# must never contain a real key. Vite exposes variables prefixed VITE_ to the
# browser bundle — anything here is PUBLIC, so the only safe value is a key that is
# safe to publish.
#
# The ingest key (drone:ingest) belongs on the Jetson, never in a browser build.
# What belongs here is the DASHBOARD key: detections:read, which grants read access
# to detections plus the review/copilot/report actions the UI already performs
# on-screen. It is the least-privileged key that makes the wired API paths live.

# Leave unset (or empty) and every call in src/lib/api.ts keeps its fixture
# fallback — the zero-key classroom demo runs with this file absent entirely.
# Set it and the dashboard talks to the backend at http://127.0.0.1:8000
# (proxied through Vite in dev — see vite.config.ts).
#
# Mint it with the `dashboard` preset, which is exactly this scope and no more
# (see SCOPE_PRESETS in backend/app/core/security.py):
#
#   cd backend && python scripts/create_api_key.py --name dashboard-laptop \
#       --preset dashboard
#
# Do NOT use --preset admin here: it can mint further keys, and this value is
# inlined into a browser bundle.
#
# Add `missions:write` (--preset operator) only if the console needs to create or
# close missions itself; the drone creates its own.
#
VITE_DASHBOARD_KEY=

# Google Maps JavaScript API key. The dashboard map is drawn with the Maps JS API,
# so this is required for the map to render at all — a missing key shows an
# explicit "map unavailable" notice rather than a blank grey rectangle.
#
# The key is inlined into the browser bundle and is PUBLIC. Restrict it by HTTP
# referrer in Google Cloud (APIs & Services -> Credentials) and enable
# "Maps JavaScript API" on the project. Never put a server key here.
#
VITE_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# Optional Map ID for cloud-styled maps (Google Cloud -> Map Management).
# Leave blank to use the built-in satellite / hybrid / roadmap map types.
#
VITE_GOOGLE_MAPS_MAP_ID=

# Live MJPEG feed served by the Jetson (jetson/stream.py, port 8080).
#
#   /jetson/stream                   same-origin, through the Vite dev proxy
#                                    (see vite.config.ts) — the default
#   http://<jetson-ip>:8080/stream   direct — WORKS IN FIREFOX, BLOCKED IN CHROME
#   (empty)                          hide the in-app feed entirely
#
# Prefer the proxied path. Chromium treats a cross-origin
# multipart/x-mixed-replace subresource as opaque and refuses it with
# ERR_BLOCKED_BY_ORB, so the direct URL paints nothing even though the request
# succeeds. The dev proxy makes it same-origin and ORB does not apply. Its target
# is AEROSHIELD_JETSON_ORIGIN in vite.config.ts.
#
# A production build has no dev proxy, so the same path must be reverse-proxied
# there too.
#
VITE_JETSON_STREAM_URL=

# Bare scheme://host:port of the Jetson itself. Used only for the "Open direct"
# escape hatch in the camera picture-in-picture, which has to be a top-level
# navigation to the aircraft and therefore cannot use the relative proxied path.
# Only needed when VITE_JETSON_STREAM_URL is relative — when it is an absolute
# URL its origin is reused automatically.
#
VITE_JETSON_DIRECT_ORIGIN=

# Survey origin — the SW corner of the mission grid, as "lat,lon".
#
# The survey is a 10 m × 10 m block on the KMIT campus, Narayanaguda, Hyderabad
# (Keshav Memorial Institute of Technology, off Narayanaguda Road). This corner is
# where the pilot stands. Given at six decimal places because four is ~11 m of
# latitude — wider than the whole block.
#
# This must agree with where the drone actually reports GPS. If it does not, the
# aircraft sits off the edge of the grid and the map has to fit both the grid and
# the drone, which pushes the effective zoom far enough out that the drone stops
# being visible. Keep it in step with the Jetson's --sim-origin.
#
VITE_SURVEY_ORIGIN=17.396947,78.490706

# The backend mission whose telemetry the dashboard follows, by call-sign.
#
# Must match the --mission-name the drone was started with:
#
#   make run MISSION=kmit-01
#
# The drone creates the mission on first contact if it does not exist, so the two
# only have to agree on the string; the dashboard resolves it to the backend's
# integer id via GET /api/missions.
#
# With this unset, the dashboard never asks the backend for a position and the
# in-process simulator stays the only source of telemetry — which is what keeps the
# zero-key classroom demo working. Note it needs VITE_DASHBOARD_KEY too: without a
# key the request is not made at all.
#
# What goes live when you set this: the aircraft marker, its altitude, speed,
# heading, battery, satellites, fix type and flight mode. Coverage, the flown track
# and the detection list stay simulated — the backend cannot answer for those yet,
# and detections arrive over their own path.
#
VITE_MISSION_NAME=
